rt they are” has been used for centuries to justify segregation, low expectations, and even eugenics. It’s biological determinism dressed up in modern imaging. Intelligence is not a fixed trait wired irreversibly by income. When we say poverty affects brain development, we’re talking about a dynamic, responsive organ—not a static IQ score that destines a child to a bottom set. Think of it like a muscle: a child living in poverty might have less opportunity to exercise certain cognitive skills, so those muscles may be underdeveloped. You don’t respond by putting them in the “weak children” exercise group where they lift lighter weights forever. You provide the graduated support and challenge to build that strength. Otherwise, you ensure they never catch up. ### What this means for your proposal Even if we accepted, for argument’s sake, that every child on free school meals had some cognitive lag, placing them all in a lower-ability group would still be malpractice because: - **It would remove the very environmental inputs proven to reverse those effects:** high expectations, exposure to advanced language and reasoning from peers, and a knowledge-rich curriculum. - **It would confirm a negative identity** (“I’m in this group because I’m poor and therefore less smart”), adding stress and stereotype threat, which further impairs cognition. - **It ignores the actual solution:** targeted interventions for specific, assessed skills—executive function training, literacy catch-up, trauma-informed teaching—that can help any student, regardless of background, without labeling them by economic status. If an article has led you to believe that children’s potential is capped by their socioeconomic background, I’d respectfully urge you to dig deeper. Read the work of researchers like Eric Jensen or Paul Howard-Jones, who write extensively on poverty and the brain but always frame it in terms of resilience, plasticity, and what schools can do to build cognitive capacity—never as a 