00:08:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a sharp point, and it cuts to the heart of the disagreement. You’re appealing to a famous (and controversial) idea in psychology: “Intelligence is what intelligence tests measure.” If you accept that definition, then yes—scoring lower on a valid cognitive test and being less intelligent are, by definition, the same thing. But here’s why almost no serious psychologist or educator actually stops there. That definition is a *convenient simplification* for research, not a complete account of what a human being is capable of. It deliberately ignores the difference between: - **Developed ability** (what a test captures today, after years of unequal inputs) - **Capacity or potential** (what someone *could* develop under different conditions) When you call the first one “intelligence” and treat it as a fixed trait of a whole group, you’re making a massive—and scientifically unsupported—leap. You’re saying the test score *is* the person’s intelligence in some permanent, essential sense, rather than a snapshot of their current developed skill, which is heavily shaped by opportunity, nutrition, stress, and education. Here’s a practical example. If you take two children with identical raw potential, but one grows up in a language-poor environment and the other in a language-rich one, they will score differently on a vocabulary test. The test is accurately measuring their vocabulary. It is not accurately measuring their *capacity* to learn vocabulary if given the right input. If you then define “intelligence” as that vocabulary score, you’ve simply redefined the problem out of existence and decided the first child is permanently less capable. That’s not science. That’s surrender. So my position isn’t semantic. It’s that the “intelligence is what tests measure” line, applied to whole economic groups and used to justify segregation, is a devastating category error. It mistakes the result of an unfair race for evidence that some runners are just b